        "Effective succession planning begins not with identifying replacement candidates but with deeply understanding what the organization actually needs at each leadership level. This requires a clear-eyed analysis of the capabilities, relationships, and cultural commitments a leader contributes -- and an honest assessment of which of those contributions are genuinely irreplaceable versus which have become irreplaceable by default, because no one else has been developed to carry them. Many leaders are irreplaceable because the organization was never designed to make them replaceable.",
        "Ram Charan, Stephen Drotter, and James Noel's 'Leadership Pipeline' model provides one of the most useful frameworks for thinking systematically about succession. Their core insight is that as leaders advance through organizational levels -- from managing self, to managing others, to managing managers, to managing a function, to managing a business -- the nature of effective leadership changes fundamentally at each transition. Most succession failures occur not because the successor lacks capability, but because they apply the skills and mindsets of their previous level to the requirements of their new one.",
