        "In 1996, a research team at Hewlett-Packard conducted an internal study on the cost of employee turnover and reached a conclusion that surprised even seasoned HR professionals: the most expensive knowledge to lose was not technical expertise documented in manuals and databases. It was the contextual judgment that experienced employees carried in their heads -- knowing when a rule applied and when it was safe to bend it, understanding which clients needed personal calls rather than automated updates, recognizing the early warning signs of a production problem before it became a crisis. This kind of knowledge -- what organizational theorists Michael Polanyi and later Nonaka and Takeuchi called tacit knowledge -- is the invisible infrastructure of every high-performing organization.",
