      "Bekzod is the founder and CEO of a regional agricultural supply company in Osh, Kyrgyzstan, that he built over eighteen years from a single-truck distribution operation to a company with 70 employees, four regional offices, and exclusive distribution agreements with three international equipment manufacturers. Bekzod is 58 years old and has been thinking about stepping back from day-to-day operations for the past three years. He has two potential internal successors: Azamat, his operations director, who has been with the company for twelve years and knows the business deeply but is perceived by the sales team as overly cautious and conflict-averse; and Gulnara, his sales director, who joined five years ago, has grown the sales team from eight to twenty people, and is widely regarded as the most commercially talented person in the company, but who has no experience managing operations, finance, or supplier relationships. Bekzod has been planning to announce a transition to Azamat in the next twelve months, primarily because of his tenure and operational expertise. However, in the past three months, two of Gulnara's best salespeople have told her privately that they will leave if Azamat becomes CEO, because they do not believe he has the vision or energy to grow the company. Bekzod has not discussed the succession plan openly with either Azamat or Gulnara, and neither knows they are being considered. He has no formal succession planning document. He has been managing all three major supplier relationships personally and has not introduced either candidate to those suppliers. One supplier has hinted that when Bekzod steps back, they will want to reassess the distribution agreement.",
